<title>EGYPT: Egypt to partially privatise battery firm.</title>
<headline>Egypt to partially privatise battery firm.</headline>
<dateline>CAIRO 1996-11-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Egyptian state's Holding Company for Chemical Industries said on Wednesday it wanted to sell 38 percent of battery maker Chloride Egypt to an anchor investor.</p>
<p>The offer is of 34,200 shares in the unlisted firm, which is wholly owned by the State Company for Batteries. Nominal value of the share is 100 pounds.</p>
<p>Bid documents can be obtained from a date to be announced shortly from the Giza branch of Commercial International Bank (CIB) or the Commercial International Company for Investment</p>
<p>Chloride Egypt manufactures batteries for vehicles, trains and computers under licence from the British firm Chloride.</p>
<p>Here are details of the financial statements for the year 94/95 and 95/96 and balance sheets at the end of the two years on March 31. (All figures are in millions of pounds)</p>
<p>					 94/95	95/96</p>
<p>NET SALES			   61.304	78.874</p>
<p>NET PROFITS			  2.671	 4.191</p>
<p>				    31MARCH95   31MARCH96</p>
<p>TOTAL LONG ASSETS		 15.993	16.726</p>
<p>of which:</p>
<p>    Fixed assets		  41.658     43.951</p>
<p>    Depreciation		 (27.668)   (30.137)</p>
<p>TOTAL CURRENT ASSETS	    25.059     39.421</p>
<p>of which:</p>
<p>Stock of finished and</p>
<p>     unfinished products	13.951     16.910</p>
<p>    Debtors			  8.697	9.860</p>
<p>    Cash at banks		  2.088     11.787</p>
<p>TOTAL CURRENT LIABILITIES     23.211     38.316</p>
